    body: "Assyria deported Israelites in stages (Tiglath-Pileser III took Galilee and Gilead c. 734–732 BC; Sargon II took Samaria 722 BC, claiming 27,290 deportees). WHERE THEY WERE TAKEN is recorded in both Assyrian and biblical sources, and they agree: Halah, the Habor river region (Gozan), and the cities of the Medes — northern Mesopotamia and northwest Iran (2 Kings 17:6; Assyrian resettlement records). Assyrian policy deliberately scattered captives among other populations to dissolve national identity. What can be traced afterward: Israelite names appear in Assyrian administrative documents from these regions for a few generations, then fade — the documented trail ends in assimilation, not in a secret intact nation.",
    deep: "This is one of the few episodes in the whole story where an outside imperial record and the biblical account line up almost point for point, which is why it earns a DOCUMENTED grade. The Assyrian side comes from royal annals. Tiglath-Pileser III's inscriptions record his western campaigns of the 730s BC, stripping territory from the northern kingdom and deporting populations from Galilee and the Transjordan. His successor Sargon II claims in his annals — preserved in the inscriptions from his palace at Dur-Sharrukin (Khorsabad) — to have carried off 27,290 people from Samaria after the city fell, a figure biblical scholars treat as a plausible administrative count of the deported urban elite rather than the total population.\n\nThe biblical text in 2 Kings 17:6 states that the king of Assyria took Israel away and settled the exiles 'in Halah and in Habor by the river of Gozan, and in the cities of the Medes.' These are real, locatable places: Gozan is Tell Halaf on the Habor (Khabur) river in what is now northeastern Syria, and the cities of the Medes lie in northwestern Iran. The convergence of an independent Assyrian source and the biblical destinations is exactly the kind of double attestation historians prize. Assyrian deportation was a deliberate imperial technology: two-way population transfers designed to break the link between a people and its land and to dissolve rebellious national identities into the wider empire.\n\nThe honest limit is what happens next, and it is the opposite of the romantic legend. Cuneiform administrative and legal tablets from centers like Gozan/Tell Halaf do preserve some personal names built on the divine element Yahweh (Yahwistic names), showing deportees or their children living and working there for a generation or two. Then that signal thins out and disappears into the ordinary onomastic mix of Mesopotamia. There is no evidence of the northern deportees maintaining a distinct corporate identity, a temple, or a continuous community the way the later Babylonian exiles of Judah did. The documented trail runs into assimilation. This is precisely the factual foundation that later 'ten lost tribes' theories overrun: the tribes were not spirited off intact to a hidden homeland; a defined slice of the population was resettled in named regions and, over a few generations, absorbed. What genuinely remains uncertain is the fraction deported versus the fraction that stayed or fled, and the fate of individual descendants once their names stopped being recorded — uncertainty about detail, not about the basic shape of the event.",
