    body: "Judah's elite and much of its population were deported to Babylonia in waves (597, 586, and c. 582 BC). Unlike the Assyrian deportees, the Judahites in Babylon KEPT their identity — settling in communities like Tel-Abib on the Chebar canal (Ezekiel 1:3), documented outside the Bible by the 'Al-Yahudu' tablets ('Judah-town'), a Babylonian archive recording exiled Judean families farming, trading, and keeping Hebrew names for generations. When Persia allowed return (538 BC), only a minority went back. The rest became the Babylonian Jewish community — which lasted 2,500 years continuously, produced the Babylonian Talmud, and only ended with the mass departure to Israel in 1950–1952. This is the best-documented ancient migration chain in the whole story.",
    deep: "The Babylonian exile is the most richly documented link in the entire chain because three independent bodies of evidence converge on it. First, the Babylonian Chronicle series — cuneiform tablets in the British Museum, including the chronicle covering Nebuchadnezzar II's early reign — records his capture of Jerusalem, naming the year the city was taken and a king deposed, which dovetails with the biblical account of the first deportation in 597 BC. Second, the so-called Jehoiachin ration tablets, administrative texts recovered from Babylon and published by the Assyriologist Ernst Weidner, list oil and grain rations issued to 'Yaukin, king of the land of Yahud' (Jehoiachin of Judah) and his sons — a captive Judahite king drawing an allowance in the heart of the empire, exactly as 2 Kings describes. Third, and most vivid, the Al-Yahudu tablets: a corpus of Babylonian legal and business documents, brought to publication in recent years, from a settlement whose name literally means 'the town of Judah.' They show deported Judean families over several generations farming crown land, paying taxes, lending and borrowing, and — crucially — continuing to give their children Yahwistic Hebrew names.\n\nThat last detail is the hinge of the whole story. Where the northern deportees to Assyria faded into assimilation, the Judahites in Babylonia preserved a corporate identity. The book of Ezekiel opens among the exiles by the Chebar canal (Ezekiel 1:3), placing a prophet inside these very communities. When Cyrus of Persia conquered Babylon and issued his decree permitting subject peoples to return home in 538 BC, the biblical books of Ezra and Nehemiah record that only a portion of the exiles actually went back to rebuild Jerusalem and the Temple.\n\nThe rest stayed, and their continuity is a documented fact of history rather than a legend. The Babylonian (later Iraqi) Jewish community endured for some two and a half millennia, becoming the great center of rabbinic learning that produced the Babylonian Talmud in late antiquity. It persisted through Persian, Islamic, Ottoman, and modern rule until it was effectively ended in the mid-twentieth century, when the overwhelming majority left for the new State of Israel in the 1950–1952 airlift known as Operation Ezra and Nehemiah, an event covered by modern press and government records. What remains debated is quantitative rather than existential — how many people were deported in each wave, and how large the 'people of the land' left behind in Judah actually were — but the migration, the destination, the preservation of identity, and the eventual return are all attested from multiple independent sides.",
