    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["diaspora"],
    body: "Not all migration was forced. A Jewish military colony with its own temple existed at Elephantine in southern Egypt by the 5th century BC (its papyri survive — contracts, letters, even correspondence with Jerusalem's governor). After Alexander (332 BC), Jews settled across the Greek world; Alexandria held one of antiquity's largest Jewish communities and produced the Septuagint translation. By the 1st century AD, Philo, Josephus, and Acts 2 all describe Jewish communities from Rome to Persia. Rough scholarly estimates put Roman-era Jews at several million, most already living OUTSIDE the land — the diaspora predates the Temple's destruction.",
    deep: "The word 'diaspora' is often paired only with catastrophe, but a large part of the Jewish spread across the ancient world was voluntary or economic, and the documentation for it is unusually good. The showcase example is Elephantine, an island in the Nile at Aswan in southern Egypt, where a Jewish military garrison served the Persian empire in the fifth century BC. The community left behind a cache of Aramaic papyri — marriage contracts, property deeds, loan agreements, and letters — that survive because of Egypt's dry climate. Remarkably, they even maintained their own temple to Yahu (a form of the divine name) and wrote to authorities in Judah for help rebuilding it after it was destroyed, corresponding with the governor of Judah and referring to the sons of Sanballat of Samaria. This is a Jewish community living far outside the land, with its own institutions, generations before any Roman dispersion.\n\nThe pace accelerated after Alexander the Great's conquests (from 332 BC) knit the eastern Mediterranean into a single Greek-speaking world. Jews settled widely, and Alexandria in Egypt grew one of the largest and most prosperous Jewish communities of antiquity. Its most famous product was the Septuagint, the translation of the Hebrew scriptures into Greek — itself proof of a Jewish population so thoroughly at home in Greek that it needed its Bible in that language. From Alexandria comes Philo, the Jewish philosopher whose treatise addressed to the emperor (the Embassy to Gaius) describes Jewish communities scattered throughout the Mediterranean and beyond.\n\nBy the first century AD the picture is corroborated from several directions. The historian Josephus, writing in Greek for a Roman audience, repeatedly refers to Jewish populations across the empire and in Parthian Mesopotamia. The New Testament book of Acts, in its Pentecost scene (Acts 2:9–11), lists Jews and proselytes present in Jerusalem 'from every nation under heaven,' naming regions from Parthia and Media to Rome and Libya — a rhetorical flourish, but one that only works because a real, far-flung diaspora existed. Modern estimates of the Jewish population under Rome run into the low millions, with the majority already living outside Judea. The historically important consequence, well supported by all this evidence, is that the diaspora is older than the Roman destruction of the Temple in 70 AD; dispersion was already the normal condition of most Jews before the great catastrophes. What remains uncertain is chiefly numerical — ancient population figures are estimates built on scattered data, and the precise size of any single community can only be approximated.",
