    tier: "DOCUMENTED",
    tags: ["deportation", "diaspora"],
    body: "After the two revolts, Rome killed or enslaved hundreds of thousands (Josephus gives 97,000 enslaved in 70 AD; slave markets reportedly glutted after 135 AD), barred Jews from Jerusalem, and renamed the province Palaestina. Jewish life continued IN the land (especially Galilee, where the Mishnah was compiled c. 200 AD) — the population was decimated and dispersed, not entirely removed. From this point the major diaspora branches slowly form: Mizrahi (Middle East/Babylonia), Sephardi (Iberia, documented from Roman times, flourishing under Islam, expelled 1492), Ashkenazi (Rhineland by the 10th century, later Eastern Europe), plus older communities in Yemen, North Africa, Ethiopia, India, and Persia.",
    deep: "Two Jewish revolts against Rome bracket this entry, and both are documented by ancient historians. The first, ending in the fall of Jerusalem and the burning of the Second Temple in 70 AD, is described in detail by Josephus, an eyewitness turned Roman client, in his Jewish War; he gives large figures for the killed and reports 97,000 taken captive and enslaved. The second, the Bar Kokhba revolt of 132–135 AD, is recorded chiefly by the Roman historian Cassius Dio, whose account (in book 69 of his Roman History) describes devastating Judean losses; ancient sources report slave markets glutted with captives afterward. In the wake of the second revolt the emperor Hadrian barred Jews from Jerusalem, refounded the city as Aelia Capitolina, and renamed the province Syria Palaestina — a deliberate erasure of the Jewish name from the map.\n\nA careful reading resists the myth of total expulsion. Jewish life did not end in the land after 135 AD; it shifted its center of gravity, above all to Galilee, where the rabbinic movement flourished. It was there, around 200 AD, that Rabbi Judah ha-Nasi is traditionally credited with compiling the Mishnah, the foundational document of rabbinic Judaism — hardly the product of an empty land. The population was decimated, enslaved, and scattered, but a continuous Jewish presence in the land persisted for centuries. This nuance is why historians describe a dispersion and demographic collapse rather than a single moment of removal.\n\nFrom this era the great branches of the diaspora take their long-term shape, each with its own trajectory and its own documentation. The Mizrahi communities of the Middle East continue the ancient Babylonian and Persian settlements. The Sephardi world of the Iberian Peninsula is attested from Roman times, flourished spectacularly under Islamic rule in medieval Spain, and was shattered by the expulsion of 1492 under the Alhambra Decree. The Ashkenazi communities appear in the Rhineland by around the tenth century and later shift eastward into Poland, Lithuania, and beyond. Older and distinct communities persist in Yemen, across North Africa, in Ethiopia, in India, and in Persia. The texture of medieval diaspora life is preserved in extraordinary detail by the Cairo Geniza, a synagogue storeroom in Old Cairo that accumulated centuries of documents — letters, contracts, and everyday records — because Jewish law discouraged discarding writings bearing the divine name. What is documented is the destruction, the renaming, the enslavements, and the continuity of communities; what remains estimated is the exact demographic scale of the losses and the precise routes by which each diaspora branch first formed.",
