    tier: "DEBATED",
    tags: ["claims"],
    body: "Ethiopia's Beta Israel practiced a Torah-based Judaism for many centuries (documented from medieval sources) and hold traditions of descent from the tribe of Dan or from Solomon and the Queen of Sheba. Genetic studies place them closest to other Ethiopian populations, suggesting large-scale local conversion in antiquity rather than mass migration — while their religious tradition is ancient and genuine, preserving practices (like animal sacrifice) lost elsewhere. Israel's rabbinate recognized them and airlifted the community to Israel (Operations Moses 1984, Solomon 1991 — 14,325 people in 36 hours, documented). Verdict: the community and its ancient Judaism are documented; the specific tribal-descent tradition cannot be verified.",
    deep: "The Beta Israel of Ethiopia are one of the most instructive cases in the section precisely because two things that are usually bundled together — the reality of a community's Judaism and the specific story of its origin — pull apart under evidence, which is why the grade is DEBATED. What is well documented is the community itself. The Beta Israel practiced a form of Judaism grounded in the Torah (the Orit in Ge'ez), observed by their own priests (kessim), for many centuries, and medieval Ethiopian sources refer to them as a distinct religious group with a long history in the highlands of the Gondar region. Notably, their practice preserved archaic features — including continued animal sacrifice tied to festivals — that had disappeared from rabbinic Judaism elsewhere, and it lacked later developments like the Talmud, indicating a tradition that branched off or developed in relative isolation. That antiquity and authenticity are not in serious dispute.\n\nThe contested part is the origin story. The Beta Israel hold traditions of descent from the lost tribe of Dan, and Ethiopian royal tradition more broadly connects the nation to a union of King Solomon and the Queen of Sheba (Makeda) producing Menelik. In the sixteenth century the eminent Egyptian rabbinic authority known as the Radbaz issued a ruling treating the Beta Israel as descendants of Dan, a decision that later carried weight in their formal recognition. Against the migration reading, genome studies place the Beta Israel closest to other Ethiopian and Northeast African populations rather than to Middle Eastern Jewish communities. Most geneticists therefore interpret the data as pointing to large-scale local conversion or adoption of Judaism in antiquity rather than a mass migration of Israelites into Ethiopia — though the deep and complex population history of the Horn of Africa leaves room for genuine scholarly disagreement, and this is not a closed question.\n\nThe modern chapter is fully documented and should not be confused with the ancient one. The State of Israel and its Chief Rabbinate recognized the Beta Israel as Jews, and the community was brought to Israel in dramatic operations: Operation Moses in 1984–85 via Sudan, and Operation Solomon in 1991, a 36-hour airlift that carried on the order of 14,000 people (commonly cited as 14,325) out of Addis Ababa during Ethiopia's civil war. Those events are recorded in government and press archives. The honest verdict separates the strands: the existence, antiquity, and genuineness of Beta Israel Judaism are documented; the specific claim of literal descent from the tribe of Dan or from Solomon cannot be verified and is not supported by the genetic evidence as it currently stands. What remains genuinely open — the reason for the DEBATED grade rather than a flat ruling — is exactly how and when Judaism took root among the ancestors of the Beta Israel, a question serious scholars still argue.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Medieval Ethiopian sources on the Beta Israel", url: null, where: "Ge'ez chronicles and later hagiographic and royal sources; surveyed in Steven Kaplan, The Beta Israel (Falasha) in Ethiopia (1992)." },
      { cite: "Genetic studies (e.g. Behar 2010 supplement)", url: null, where: "D.M. Behar et al., Nature 466 (2010) and related population-genetics literature placing Beta Israel closest to other Ethiopian populations." },
      { cite: "Operation Solomon records (1991)", url: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Solomon", where: "The 1991 airlift of Ethiopian Jews to Israel; Wikipedia overview with contemporary press and government references." }
    ]
